","We are given the definite integral
\[ \int_{-1}^{2} [ (x^2 + 3) - 1 ] \, dx. \]
First, we simplify the expression inside the integral:
$$ (x^2 + 3) - 1 = x^2 + 2. $$
Now, we evaluate the integral:
$$ \int_{-1}^{2} (x^2 + 2) \, dx = \left[ \frac{1}{3}x^3 + 2x \right_{-1}^{2} $$
We evaluate the antiderition-integrated function at the upper upper and lower limits:
$$ \left( \frac{1}{3}(2)^3 + 2(2) \right) - \left( \frac{1}{3}(-1)^3 + 2(-1) \right = \left( \frac{1}{3}(8) + 4 \right) - \left( \frac{-1}{3} - 2 \right $$
$$ = \left( \frac{8}{3} + 4 \1\right - \left( -\frac{1}{3} - 2 \right = \frac{8}{3} + 4 + \frac{1}{3} + 2 = \frac{9}{3} + 6 = 3 + 6 = 9 $$
Therefore,
$$ \int_{-1}^{2} [ (x^2 + 3) - 1 ] \, dx = 9. $$

